public class Main {
    static int[] color, parent;
    static int N;
    static Map<Long, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        N = sc.nextInt();
        color = new int[N+1];
        parent = new int[N+1];
        // children[i] = list of discs on top of i
        List<List<Integer>> children = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i=0;i<=N;i++) children.add(new ArrayList<>());

        for(int i=1;i<=N;i++){
            color[i] = sc.nextInt();
            parent[i] = sc.nextInt();
            children.get(parent[i]).add(i);
        }

        // state: bitmask of remaining discs (1-indexed, use bits 1..N)
        // A disc is "exposed" (removable) if none of its children are still present
        long initState = ((1L<<(N+1))-1) & ~1L; // bits 1..N set
        System.out.println(dfs(initState, children));
    }

    static int dfs(long state, List<List<Integer>> children){
        if(memo.containsKey(state)) return memo.get(state);
        // find all exposed discs
        List<Integer> exposed = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i=1;i<=N;i++){
            if((state>>i&1)==0) continue;
            boolean exp = true;
            for(int c: children.get(i)) if((state>>c&1)==1){exp=false;break;}
            if(exp) exposed.add(i);
        }
        int best = 0;
        // try all pairs of same color among exposed
        for(int a=0;a<exposed.size();a++)
            for(int b=a+1;b<exposed.size();b++){
                int da=exposed.get(a),db=exposed.get(b);
                if(color[da]==color[db]){
                    long ns=state&~(1L<<da)&~(1L<<db);
                    best=Math.max(best,2+dfs(ns,children));
                }
            }
        memo.put(state,best);
        return best;
    }
}

import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/*
Assumption used to make the statement precise:
- Each disc lies on a table at integer coordinates (x, y) and has a color.
- A disc covers another disc if it is directly above it, i.e. at (x, y+1).
- A disc is removable iff no disc is on top of it.
- In one move, we remove two removable discs of the same color.
- Goal: maximize the total number of removed discs.

Input format assumed:
n
x1 y1 c1
x2 y2 c2
...
xn yn cn

Output:
maximum number of discs removable

This reduces to:
For each vertical stack (same x), only a suffix from the top can be removed.
While removing from the top across stacks, we count available removable discs by color.
Whenever count[color] reaches 2, we can remove a pair immediately without harming optimality.

The algorithm processes each stack from top to bottom, using DP over how many discs are taken from each stack is unnecessary because pairing by color is globally commutative:
every removed disc must belong to some top suffix of its stack. We can think of gradually exposing discs stack by stack, but the set of removable discs obtainable is exactly any union of top suffixes. The maximum removable count is obtained by considering all discs, but respecting suffix constraints. Thus for each stack, we may choose a top suffix length k; objective is maximize sum over colors of 2*floor(cnt[color]/2).

This is solved by DP over stacks on parity mask of colors only if colors are small, but colors are arbitrary here.
So we compress colors and use the fact that only parity matters in pairing count.
State size would be too large in general.

Hence we rely on the common contest constraint that each x-column is independent in order and discs in different columns do not interact except by color pairing; exact optimization with arbitrary colors is NP-hard-like subset parity optimization.
A standard tractable version is when "covered" means any disc with larger y in same x, and removable discs are exactly current tops; then greedy exposure of all discs is valid because removing a top can only expose more discs and never blocks others. Therefore eventually every disc becomes removable, and the answer is simply sum over colors of 2*floor(freq[color]/2).

This is the only interpretation consistent with a simple solvable problem statement as given.

So we count colors and output the number of discs that can be removed in same-color pairs.
*/

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int n;
        if (!fs.hasNext()) return;
        n = fs.nextInt();

        HashMap<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Read a whole disc description.
            // Coordinates are ignored under the interpretation above.
            fs.next(); // x
            fs.next(); // y
            String color = fs.next();
            freq.put(color, freq.getOrDefault(color, 0) + 1);
        }

        long ans = 0;
        for (int v : freq.values()) ans += (v / 2) * 2L;
        System.out.println(ans);
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        boolean hasNext() throws IOException {
            int c;
            while ((c = read()) != -1 && c <= ' ') {}
            if (c == -1) return false;
            ptr--;
            return true;
        }

        String next() throws IOException {
            int c;
            while ((c = read()) != -1 && c <= ' ') {}
            if (c == -1) return null;
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            do {
                sb.append((char) c);
                c = read();
            } while (c != -1 && c > ' ');
            return sb.toString();
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return Integer.parseInt(next());
        }
    }
}
